# Target C-II — unit-force equilibrium forces a five- or six-vertex reciprocal hull

**Date:** 2026-09-18  
**Status:** proved candidate; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** the six-point unit-force reciprocal `K_6` forced by a degree-pure minimal one-anchor `K_5` dual block  
**Depends on:** `target_c_one_anchor_k5_reciprocal_unit_force_2026-09-18.md`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The reciprocal reduction gives six points `y_0,...,y_5` in general position and signs
`s_ij in {+1,-1}` such that at every reciprocal vertex

\[
\sum_{j\ne i}s_{ij}{y_i-y_j\over |y_i-y_j|}=0.
\tag{0.1}
\]

The previous turn-gap note used source pentagon geometry to show that every non-ground
reciprocal hull angle is greater than `pi/3`.  The unit-force equilibrium itself gives a
much stronger bound, applies also at the ground point, and sharply constrains the local
sign split.

Put

\[
a:=\arccos(1/4),
\qquad
\beta:=\arccos(-1/4)=\pi-a,
\qquad
\alpha:=2\arccos(1/4)=2a.
\tag{0.2}
\]

Numerically,

\[
\beta\approx104.477512^\circ,
\qquad
\alpha\approx151.044976^\circ.
\]

The exact conclusions are:

1. every reciprocal hull angle is strictly greater than `beta`;
2. a hull vertex whose incident edge signs split `1+4` has hull angle strictly greater
   than `alpha`;
3. the reciprocal convex hull has exactly five or six vertices;
4. if it has five vertices, every hull vertex has a `2+3` sign split;
5. if it has six vertices, at most one hull vertex has a `1+4` sign split.

Thus the finite reciprocal target has only two order-type regimes left: a convex
pentagon plus one interior point, with `2+3` signs at every hull vertex, or a convex
hexagon in which at least five vertices have `2+3` signs.

---

## 1. A sharp three-vector resultant bound

Let three unit vectors have arguments

\[
0\le x\le z<\pi.
\]

Their squared resultant is

\[
\begin{aligned}
|1+e^{ix}+e^{iz}|^2
&=3+2\bigl(\cos x+\cos z+\cos(z-x)\bigr)\\
&=3+2\Bigl(\cos z+2\cos(z/2)\cos(x-z/2)\Bigr).
\end{aligned}
\]

Because `|x-z/2|<=z/2<pi/2`,

\[
\cos(x-z/2)\ge\cos(z/2),
\]

and therefore

\[
\boxed{
|1+e^{ix}+e^{iz}|^2\ge5+4\cos z.
}
\tag{1.1}
\]

If the three directions are distinct, the first inequality is strict.

Consequently, if three distinct unit vectors lie in an angular interval of width at
most `phi<pi`, then

\[
\boxed{
|u_1+u_2+u_3|>\sqrt{5+4\cos\phi}.
}
\tag{1.2}
\]

The endpoint expression is sharp as an infimum: two of the three vectors may approach
one endpoint while the third approaches the other.

---

## 2. Two-versus-three equilibrium forces `phi>beta`

Fix a reciprocal hull vertex `y_i`.  The five geometric rays from `y_i` to the other
five reciprocal points lie in the open hull cone of angle `phi_i<pi`.  Reversing all
rays does not change their angular span, so (0.1) is an equality between two signed
subsets of five unit vectors in an interval of width `phi_i`.

If the incident signs split `2+3`, move one sign class to the other side.  The sum of
the two minority vectors equals the sum of the three majority vectors.  The minority
resultant has norm at most two, whereas (1.2) gives

\[
2
>
\sqrt{5+4\cos\phi_i}.
\]

Hence

\[
4>5+4\cos\phi_i,
\qquad
\cos\phi_i<-{1\over4},
\]

and since `0<phi_i<pi`,

\[
\boxed{
\phi_i>\arccos(-1/4)=\beta.
}
\tag{2.1}
\]

The strictness is load-bearing and comes from general position: the three majority rays
are distinct, because no three reciprocal points are collinear.

---

## 3. One-versus-four equilibrium forces `phi>alpha`

If the incident signs split `1+4`, the four-vector resultant has norm exactly one.
Let `n` be the unit direction along the bisector of the hull cone.  Every one of the
four majority unit vectors has projection at least `cos(phi_i/2)` onto `n`.  Therefore

\[
1
=
|u_1+u_2+u_3+u_4|
\ge
n\cdot(u_1+u_2+u_3+u_4)
>
4\cos(\phi_i/2).
\tag{3.1}
\]

The last inequality is strict because four distinct directions cannot all lie on the
two boundary rays of the cone.  Thus

\[
\cos(\phi_i/2)<1/4,
\]

so

\[
\boxed{
\phi_i>2\arccos(1/4)=\alpha.
}
\tag{3.2}
\]

An all-equal `0+5` sign split is impossible at a hull vertex: all five rays have
strictly positive projection on the cone bisector, so their signed sum cannot vanish.
Thus every hull vertex is of `2+3` or `1+4` type.

---

## 4. The reciprocal hull has five or six vertices

Because `a=arccos(1/4)<pi/2`,

\[
\beta=\pi-a>{\pi\over2}.
\tag{4.1}
\]

Every hull angle is strictly greater than `beta`.  A convex quadrilateral would
therefore have angle sum strictly greater than

\[
4\beta>2\pi,
\]

contradicting its exact angle sum `2pi`.  Triangles are excluded a fortiori.

There are only six reciprocal points, so

\[
\boxed{
|\operatorname{vert}(\operatorname{conv}\{y_0,\ldots,y_5\})|\in\{5,6\}.
}
\tag{4.2}
\]

This strengthens the earlier statement that only a reciprocal triangle with three
non-ground vertices is impossible: no reciprocal triangle or quadrilateral can occur
at all, including those containing the ground point.

---

## 5. A five-vertex hull has no `1+4` sign vertex

Suppose the reciprocal hull is a pentagon.  Its angle sum is `3pi`.

If even one hull vertex had a `1+4` sign split, Sections 2--3 would give

\[
\sum_{v\in\partial H}\phi_v
>
\alpha+4\beta
=
2a+4(\pi-a)
=
4\pi-2a.
\tag{5.1}
\]

But `a<pi/2`, so

\[
4\pi-2a>3\pi,
\]

contradicting the pentagon angle sum.  Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\text{if the reciprocal hull has five vertices, every hull vertex has a `2+3` sign split.}
}
\tag{5.2}
\]

Equivalently, if `d_i` denotes the number of positive reciprocal edge signs incident to
a hull vertex, then

\[
\boxed{d_i\in\{2,3\}\quad\text{at all five hull vertices}.}
\tag{5.3}
\]

The sixth, interior reciprocal point is not constrained by this hull-cone argument.

---

## 6. A six-vertex hull has at most one `1+4` sign vertex

Now suppose all six reciprocal points are hull vertices.  The angle sum is `4pi`.
If two vertices had `1+4` sign splits, then the remaining four vertices have at least
the `2+3` lower bound, so

\[
\sum_{v\in\partial H}\phi_v
>
2\alpha+4\beta
=
4a+4(\pi-a)
=
4\pi,
\tag{6.1}
\]

again impossible.

Hence

\[
\boxed{
\text{on a convex reciprocal hexagon, at most one vertex has a `1+4` sign split.}
}
\tag{6.2}
\]

At least five vertices therefore satisfy `d_i in {2,3}`.

---

## 7. Frontier effect

The minimal one-anchor `K_5` dual block no longer leaves an arbitrary six-point order
type or arbitrary signed `K_6`.

Any surviving reciprocal equilibrium must lie in one of exactly two geometric regimes:

- **H5:** five reciprocal points form the convex hull and one point is interior; all
  five hull vertices have signed degree `2` or `3`;
- **H6:** all six reciprocal points are in convex position; at least five vertices have
  signed degree `2` or `3`, and at most one has signed degree `1` or `4`.

The proof uses only the unit-force reciprocal equilibrium and reciprocal general
position.  It does not use the source `<120` force-gap theorem, so that theorem remains
available as an independent filter on the five non-ground vertices.

A next finite attack can therefore enumerate only H5/H6 signed order types, then impose
the source turn-gap and translation-surface constraints.  The H5 regime also admits an
additional metric exclusion recorded separately for the pentagram sign class.

No theorem-state file is changed.

**Disposition:** strengthened the reciprocal finite target from an arbitrary general-
position six-point configuration to a five- or six-vertex convex hull, with exact local
sign-split restrictions `2+3` everywhere on H5 and at at least five vertices on H6.
C-II remains open.